m trees my friends  are why you love palm trees they're so  tired of palm trees they grew up a palm  trees was still to this day I just like  that's the most beautiful tree and I've  ever seen the house was on fire what  what would I what would I grab first my  dad has a it's a 19 I believe 47 Gibson  master town that he gave me it was a  birthday present I had no idea that this  big box came in the mail and opened it  up and it was his his banjo this prized  possession they had forever in fact my  mom when she went in labor with me they  were at this Music Festival and he was  playing this banjo it's just really a  sentimental piece that's something  always hold you know dear to the heart  there's just beauty in the instruments  and what what they're capable of in the  stories behind them and it's natural  artwork I mean it just it's always it's  always going to be cool it's it's sort  of effortless design if you will it's  not contrived you don't really have to  try you hang a guitar on the wall and it  just it's just cool the art I have is  all extremely personally I think it  should be for people it needs to mean  something to you I can show you  something that's cool but it's got to  mean something to you so actually  everything and I have you know means  something really significant to me  things that come they come from  everywhere a lot of the instruments I  brought from from Indiana I packed my  parents sedan and drove cross-country  for three four days a lot of the stuff  comes from flea markets the Rose Bowl is  some of the most amazing offerings of  all things vintage you know buy and sell  trade stuff a lot as well I love to when  I travel always stopping in at little  thrift stores the large piece here on  the gallery walls it's the first time I  ever spit money on art a good friend of  mine Gary Widener is a broke college kid  and I threw him five hundred dollars for  painting  the time was a lot of money for me but  it said something about our friendship  and said something about how much I  believed in what he was doing and his  talent his skill and it really just  spoke to me the best camera that that  one could own is one that's you know  with you at all times regardless what a  dish it was just something about the  contrast of this black and white tree  rings I just love the center focus of  the trunk I just thought it'd be a good  idea to slice it down the middle and  separate in two portraits above the  couch the the view here is really  special  I found the space and it wasn't finished  and I came in put a deposit on it but it  instead of two to three weeks to move in  it turned out to be three months so  while they were working on it I would  come up and I would sit in the loft and  just kind of look out over the city it  was just I don't know it's kind of  refuge for me it so it's a little bit  weird I would grab my coffee in the  morning just come here there are no  blinds no window coverings it's actually  quite private up there but it just has  the most epic sunsets and the moon is  incredible at night it actually wakes me  up sometimes it's so bright it's just  like this sweet just beautiful place of  refuge so tranquil it just it's a place  to come home to and feel that peace and  and relax
 

